--- !u!114 &-2174907467296253510
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 1
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 456c7c2b477e4253b9e4faf9ed4b18a9, type: 3}
  m_Name: InstructionsParagraph
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Unity.Tutorials.Editor::Unity.Tutorials.Editor.Paragraphs.InstructionsParagraph
  m_MaskingSettings:
    m_MaskingEnabled: 0
    m_UnmaskedViews: []
    MaskPreset: {fileID: 0}
  Title:
    m_Untranslated: Instructions
  Text:
    m_Untranslated: "<b>1.</b>  In the <b>Project</b> window, navigate to <b>_Unity Essentials</b> > <b>Materials</b> > <b>Skyboxes.</b>\n\nThe skybox currently in your scene is <b>SkyBrightCloudy.</b>\n\n<b>2.</b>  Browse the other skybox materials and preview them in the <b>Preview</b> panel at the bottom of the <b>Inspector</b> window. \n\n<b>3.</b>  Click and drag any skybox that interests you directly onto the sky in the <b>Scene</b> view to see how it looks behind the room until you find one that you like. \n\n<b>4.</b> At the top of the editor click on <b>Window</b> > <b>Rendering</b> > <b>Lighting</b>. In the Lighting window, under <b>Scene</b>, click <b>Generate Lighting</b>. This will bake lighting and refresh reflections so the new skybox is used across the scene and in reflective surfaces (like the windows).\n\n<b>Note:</b>  Remember to save your scene often with <b>Ctrl+S</b> (macOS: <b>Cmd+S</b>)."
  m_CriteriaCompletion: 0
  m_Criteria:
    m_Items: []
